Let $\Lambda$ denote the set of terms of the ordinary untyped lambda calculus, with terms built from variables by abstraction $\lambda x.M$ and application $MN$ modulo $\alpha$-equivalence. Let $\lambda 2$ (the second-order, or polymorphic, lambda calculus, also known as System F) be the Curry-style type assignment system whose types are generated by the grammar
$$\sigma, \tau ::= \alpha \mid \sigma \to \tau \mid \forall \alpha.\, \sigma,$$
where $\alpha$ ranges over a countable set of type variables, and whose type-assignment rules are:
- (var) $\Gamma, x{:}\sigma \vdash x : \sigma$;
- ($\to$I) if $\Gamma, x{:}\sigma \vdash M : \tau$ then $\Gamma \vdash \lambda x.M : \sigma \to \tau$;
- ($\to$E) if $\Gamma \vdash M : \sigma \to \tau$ and $\Gamma \vdash N : \sigma$ then $\Gamma \vdash MN : \tau$;
- ($\forall$I) if $\Gamma \vdash M : \sigma$ and $\alpha$ is not free in $\Gamma$, then $\Gamma \vdash M : \forall \alpha.\sigma$;
- ($\forall$E) if $\Gamma \vdash M : \forall \alpha.\sigma$ then $\Gamma \vdash M : \sigma[\tau/\alpha]$ for any type $\tau$.

Call a closed term $M \in \Lambda$ typable in $\lambda 2$ if there exists a type $\sigma$ such that $\vdash M : \sigma$ is derivable. Determine whether the typability problem for $\lambda 2$,
$$\{ M \in \Lambda : M \text{ is typable in } \lambda 2 \},$$
is decidable.

